      "text": "Given the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\), we begin by simplifying each term.\n\nFirst, note that:\n\\[\n10x + 2 = 2(5x + 1) \n\\]\n\\[\n10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3) \n\\]\n\\[\n5x + 5 = 5(x + 1) \n\\]\n\nNow, consider these two forms and multiply them:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = [2(5x+1)][2(5x+3)][5(x+1)]\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the product, we have:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 2(5x+1) \\cdot 2(5x+3) \\cdot 5(x+1) = 2^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\nFurther simplify the expression:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 20 (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\nExpress the product as:\n\\[\n20 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) differ by 2. We can pair the integers \\((5x+1)\\) and \\((5x+3)\\) with \\(x+1\\):\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3) = (5x+1+4)(5x+1-4) = (5x+1+4)(5x+1-4) = (5x+1 + 4)(5x+1 - 4)\n\\]\n\nEach pair contributes:\n\\[\n(5x+1+2+3)(5x+1-3) = (5x+1+5)(5x-2)\n\\]\n\nFactor \\(5x+1\\):\n\\[\n(5x+1+5)(5x-2) = (5(x + 1))(5(x - \\frac{2}{5})) = 25(5x+5-1)(x-\\frac{2}{5}) = 25(5x+5-1)(x-\\frac{2}{5})\n\\]\n\nCalculate the expression:\n\\[\n20 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (5x+5-1)(x-\\frac{2}{5})\n\\]\n\nSimplify \\(20 \\cdot 25 \\):\n\\[\n500 \\cdot 5x + 500 - 20 \\cdot 5x + 500 = 500 \\cdot 5x + 500 - 100x + 500 = 500 + 500 = 1000\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1000}\n\\]",
