      "text": "To find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) for any odd \\(x\\), we start by expressing the factors in a more manageable form.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out common terms from each binomial:\n\\[\n(10x+2) = 2(5x+1)\n\\]\n\\[\n(10x+6) = 2(5x+3)\n\\]\n\\[\n5x+5 = 5(x+1)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these factorizations back into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 2(5x+1) \\cdot 2(5x+3) \\cdot 5(x+1)\n\\]\n\nNow, we can simplify this expression:\n\\[\n= 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 20 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the largest integer that always divides this expression for any odd \\(x\\). Let's analyze the expression modulo 2, 3, and 5 to find a common divisor.\n\n### Modulo 2:\nFor any odd \\(x\\), both \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) are even, and thus divisible by 2. Therefore:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3) \\equiv 2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\pmod{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n(x+1) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\n\\]\nSo, \\(2\\) divides the expression.\n\n### Modulo 3:\nFor any odd \\(x\\), \\(5x+1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}\\) and \\(5x+3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3) \\equiv 2 \\cdot 2 = 4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n(x+1) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSo, \\(3\\) does not divide the expression.\n\n### Modulo 5:\nFor any odd \\(x\\), \\(5x+1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\), \\(5x+3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\\), and \\(x+1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3) \\equiv 1 \\cdot 3 = 3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n(x+1) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSo, \\(5\\) does not divide the expression.\n\nFrom the above analysis, the only common divisor that always divides the expression is \\(2\\). Therefore, the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) for any odd \\(x\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{20}\n\\]",
